#{[17592186134083 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(-1,-2)$ and $(2,4).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =⟨(-1+t(2-(-1))),\\hspace{0.222em}(-2+t(4-(-2)))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3t-1,\\hspace{0.222em}6t-2⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6t-2)=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=xy$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =(3t-1)\\cdot (6t-2) \\\\  & =18{t}^{2}-12t+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(18{t}^{2}-12t+2)\\cdot 6\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =12{\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}-6t+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186134084 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $2$ and $3,$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}t,\\hspace{0.222em}3\\operatorname{sin}t⟩,\\hspace{2em}0\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{2y}{x}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =\\frac{2\\cdot 3\\operatorname{sin}t}{2\\operatorname{cos}t} \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{tan}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(3\\operatorname{tan}t)\\cdot (3\\operatorname{cos}t)\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}9\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-9\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =(-9\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right))-(-9\\operatorname{cos}(0)) \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186134085 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ can be parameterized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨5\\operatorname{cos}t,\\hspace{0.222em}4\\operatorname{sin}t⟩,\\hspace{2em}\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x-y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =5\\operatorname{cos}t-4\\operatorname{sin}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }(5\\operatorname{cos}t-4\\operatorname{sin}t)(-5\\operatorname{sin}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-5{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }(5\\operatorname{cos}t-4\\operatorname{sin}t)\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186134086 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $4$ centered at $O.$ It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}t,\\hspace{0.222em}4\\operatorname{sin}t⟩,\\hspace{2em}0\\le t\\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=xy,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =(4\\operatorname{cos}t)(4\\operatorname{sin}t) \\\\  & =16\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }16\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t\\cdot 4\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =64{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}t{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186134129 "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $\\mathbf{F}$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Γ}={∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\mathbf{F}(r(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t),\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t),\\,z=1-{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\operatorname{cos}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}(\\pi t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-{t}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =(\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\operatorname{cos}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot (-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t)\\cdot (-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t))+(-\\operatorname{cos}(\\pi t))\\cdot \\pi \\operatorname{cos}(\\pi t)+0\\cdot (-2t) \\\\  & =-\\pi {\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}(\\pi t)-\\pi {\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}(\\pi t) \\\\  & =-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{Γ}={∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\mathbf{F}(r(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-\\pi )\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\pi [t]_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\pi (1-(-1)) \\\\  & =-2\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186134131 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the paraboloid $z=10-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and the boundary of our infinite vertical column. One such surface is our paraboloid $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=10-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(10-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(10-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into expression for $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨-2z,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩{|}_{z=10-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨-2(10-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-20}_{P},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{0}_{Q},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{0}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-20)\\cdot (-2x)-0\\cdot (-2y)+0)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4{x}^{3}+4x{y}^{2}-40x)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/c1/c181e209cca01a1d6e372caa7f7810a5fa4aa9f177595bd92f937a7cba8b8a75.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(4{x}^{3}+4x{y}^{2}-40x)\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}\\,4{x}^{3}+4x{y}^{2}-40x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186134132 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}-t,\\,y=t-{t}^{3},\\,z=2{t}^{2}-2t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(t-{t}^{3})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+({t}^{2}-t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-{t}^{3})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}-2t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(2t-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(1-3{t}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(4t-2)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =((t-{t}^{3})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+({t}^{2}-t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})\\cdot ((2t-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(1-3{t}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(4t-2)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =(t-{t}^{3})\\cdot (2t-1)+0\\cdot (1-3{t}^{2})+({t}^{2}-t)\\cdot (4t-2) \\\\  & =t-4{t}^{2}+5{t}^{3}-2{t}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\mathbf{F}(r(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(t-4{t}^{2}+5{t}^{3}-2{t}^{4})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{4{t}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{5{t}^{3}}{4}-\\frac{2{t}^{5}}{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{60}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186134213 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=f(\\mathbf{r}(b))-f(\\mathbf{r}(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we define $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=\\underbrace{\\frac{x}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}_{P(x,y)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\underbrace{\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}_{Q(x,y)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ (if it exists) such that $\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}f.$ Notice that the domain of $\\mathbf{F}$ is ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-\\frac{2xy}{(1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ there exists a potential function $f$ such that $\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}f,$ and we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{x}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}).\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $\\mathrm{∇}f=\\mathbf{F}.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. A suitable parametrization of the curve $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨t,{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}(t+1)⟩,\\hspace{1em}t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩, \\\\ r(1) & =⟨1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & =f(1,\\operatorname{ln}2)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}(1+{1}^{2}+{\\operatorname{ln}}^{2}2)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}(1+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}(2+{\\operatorname{ln}}^{2}2)-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}(2+{\\operatorname{ln}}^{2}2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186134236 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=f(\\mathbf{r}(b))-f(\\mathbf{r}(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨t,\\operatorname{cos}t+{t}^{3}-2⟩,\\hspace{1em}t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,-1⟩, \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi ,{\\pi }^{3}-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,-1)$ and $(\\pi ,{\\pi }^{3}-3).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & =f(\\mathbf{r}(\\pi ))-f(\\mathbf{r}(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\pi ,{\\pi }^{3}-3)-f(0,0) \\\\  & ={\\pi }^{2}\\cdot ({\\pi }^{3}-3)-{0}^{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & ={\\pi }^{2}({\\pi }^{3}-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186134566 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=f(\\mathbf{r}(b))-f(\\mathbf{r}(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}f.$ Since we're given that $\\mathbf{F}$ is conservative, we know that such a function exists. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =x{y}^{2}, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & ={x}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $\\mathrm{∇}f=\\mathbf{F}.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. The position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,1⟩, \\\\ r(1) & =⟨2,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,1)$ and $(2,1).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & =f(2,1)-f(0,1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {2}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {0}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =2-0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186134567 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=f(\\mathbf{r}(b))-f(\\mathbf{r}(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0,0⟩ \\\\ r\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =⟨1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0,0)$ and $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & =f(\\mathbf{r}(\\pi ))-f(\\mathbf{r}(0)) \\\\  & =f(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi )-f(0,0,0) \\\\  & =(1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}\\pi )-(0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}0+0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}0) \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot (-1))-0 \\\\  & =1-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186152431 "The diagram shows an ellipse centered at the origin, with horizontal radius $3$ and vertical radius $5$. A parametrization is\n\n$$\nx=3\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=5\\sin\\theta,\\qquad0\\le\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$\n\nIndeed, these coordinates satisfy $x^2/9+y^2/25=1$ and trace the entire ellipse. Swapping the radii would produce a horizontally stretched ellipse."] [17592186152795 "The dashed lines locate the center at $(2,1)$. The horizontal endpoints are $x=-2$ and $x=6$, while the vertical endpoints are $y=-1$ and $y=3$. Hence the radii are $4$ horizontally and $2$ vertically. Therefore\n\n$$\nx=4\\cos\\theta+2,\\qquad y=2\\sin\\theta+1,\\qquad0\\le\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$"] [17592186171710 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S \n=\n\\iint\\limits_D f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\left\\|  \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\right\\|  \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\| = 2\\sqrt{2}u.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =(z-y)\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{x={u}^{2}\\,y=-v\\,z=v} \\\\  & =v-(-v) \\\\  & =2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}4\\sqrt{2}uv\\hspace{0.167em}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186171711 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S \n=\n\\iint\\limits_D f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\left\\|  \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\right\\|  \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\left\\langle u+v, \\: u-v, \\: v\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u-v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u-v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}-2⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖ & =‖⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}-2⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =x-y\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{x=u+v\\,y=u-v\\,z=v} \\\\  & =(u+v)-(u-v) \\\\  & =2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we denote \n\n$$\nD = \\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq u \\leq 3, \\: 0 \\leq v \\leq 3\\},\n$$\n\nour surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}(x-y)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}2v\\sqrt{6}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}2v\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{3}2v\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{3}1\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\cdot [{v}^{2}]_{0}^{3}\\cdot [u]_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\cdot 9\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =27\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186172275 "At the three indicated points, evaluate the components of $F(x,y)=\\langle x^2,-2y\\rangle$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(-2,1)&=\\langle4,-2\\rangle,\\\\\nF(2,3)&=\\langle4,-6\\rangle,\\\\\nF(0,-2)&=\\langle0,4\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matching diagram has a right-and-down arrow from each of the first two points and an upward arrow from $(0,-2)$, with the displayed component displacements."] [17592186172276 "Every arrow points downward. Its horizontal component points left for $x<0$, vanishes on the $y$-axis, and points right for $x>0$. These features match\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=xi-j=\\langle x,-1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nThe constant vector fields would have the same direction everywhere, while $-xi+yj$ would point upward in the upper half-plane and inward horizontally."] [17592186172277 "We notice the following:\n\n- The horizontal component (i.e., the $\\mathbf{i}$ component) is negative for each vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=\\mathbf{i}-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{H}(x,y)=\\mathbf{i}\n$$\n\n- The vertical component (i.e., the $\\mathbf{j}$ component) is positive for each vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{K}(x,y)=-\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $\\mathbf{G}(x,y)=-\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}.$"] [17592186172282 "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $x$-axis. So, the $\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{k}$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{H}(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k},\\hspace{2em}\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *positive* $x$-direction, so the $\\mathbf{i}$ component of our vector field is positive. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{G}(x,y,z)=-\\mathbf{i}\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $\\mathbf{i}$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{K}(x,y,z)={e}^{x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{i}.$"] [17592186172284 "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $z$-axis. So, the $\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf{j}$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{G}(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{k}\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *positive* $z$-direction, so the $\\mathbf{k}$ component of our vector field is positive. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{H}(x,y,z)=-\\mathbf{k}\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $\\mathbf{k}$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $\\mathbf{K}(x,y,z)=2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.$"] [17592186172292 "Region I is a filled ellipse, so it is convex and therefore simply connected. Region II is the plane with the entire line $y=x$ removed; it splits into the two disjoint half-planes $y>x$ and $y<x$, so it is not connected and hence not simply connected. Region III is the full plane, which is convex and simply connected. Thus I and III only are simply connected."] [17592186172375 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}v⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 0 & v \\\\ 0 & 1 & u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & v \\\\ 1 & u\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & v \\\\ 0 & u\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-v,\\hspace{0.222em}-u,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖ & =‖⟨-v,\\hspace{0.222em}-u,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-v)^{2}+(-u)^{2}+(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =(xyz)\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{x=u\\,y=v\\,z=uv} \\\\  & =u\\cdot v\\cdot uv \\\\  & ={u}^{2}{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}{u}^{2}{v}^{2}\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}+1}\\hspace{0.167em}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186172376 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖=\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =(x+y+z)\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{x={u}^{2}\\,y=u-{v}^{2}\\,z={v}^{2}} \\\\  & ={u}^{2}+(u-{v}^{2})+{v}^{2} \\\\  & ={u}^{2}+u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}2({u}^{2}+u)v\\sqrt{1+8{u}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186179426 "A function $\\psi(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $\\mathbf F= P\\,\\mathbf{i} + Q\\,\\mathbf{j}$ if the following equations hold:\n\n$$\nP = \\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial y},\\qquad Q = -\\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial x}\n$$\n\nComputing the partial derivatives of the function $\\psi(x,y),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y, \\\\ Q & =-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field with the stream function $\\psi(x,y) = e^{x} \\cos{y}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(x,y) & =P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186179483 "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\n![](images/ff/ff84680c55f5349431cf3d70066eeaba2c680569faa3b2210e5d14ef63ccd99e.png)\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}(8y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+4x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot (\\frac{x}{2}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{y}{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}4xy+2xy\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}6xy\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $6xy.$"] [17592186179484 "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s\n=\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_1 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_2 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_3} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_3 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_4} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_4 \\: \\textrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides $C_i$ and unit vectors $\\mathbf n_i$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/1d/1de4c4c55784b4a84a0493a4cc65082c430cadcc39c3d808001b2fd27665e968.png)\n\nFirst, consider the side $C_1.$ We have that $\\mathbf n_1 = \\mathbf i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(2x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot \\mathbf{i}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side $C_1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\mathbf i + t\\,\\mathbf j, \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad \\mathbf r'(t) = \\mathbf j, \\qquad t\\in [-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & =2{\\int }_{-1}^{1}1\\cdot ||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2(1-(-1)) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integrals over the sides $C_2, C_3,$ and $C_4$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = 4\\cdot 4 = 16.\n$$"] [17592186179485 "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s\n=\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_1 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_2 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_3} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_3 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_4} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_4 \\: \\textrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides $C_i$ and unit vectors $\\mathbf n_i$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/79/793755cda5243dbb427d8722c21ab150a4821c3957f0ccd0ef0fedbbd576e924.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages. \n\n- First, consider the side $C_1.$ We have that $\\mathbf n_1 = \\mathbf i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(5x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-3y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot \\mathbf{i}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side $C_1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = 2\\mathbf i + t\\,\\mathbf j, \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad \\mathbf r'(t) = \\mathbf j, \\qquad t\\in [-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & =5{\\int }_{-1}^{1}2\\cdot ||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =10{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =10\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side $C_3$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_1 \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_{C_3} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_3 \\: \\textrm{d}s = 20.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the side $C_2.$ We have that $\\mathbf n_2 = \\mathbf j,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}(5x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-3y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot \\mathbf{j}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side $C_2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = t\\,\\mathbf i + \\mathbf j, \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad \\mathbf r'(t) = \\mathbf i, \\qquad t\\in [-2,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & =-3{\\int }_{-2}^{2}1\\cdot ||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side $C_4$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_2 \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_{C_4} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_4 \\: \\textrm{d}s = -12.\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\textrm d s= 2 \\cdot 20 + 2 \\cdot (-12) = 16.\n$$"] [17592186179524 "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r} = \\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r}\n+\n \\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r}.\n$$\n\nWe're given the value of the first integral, so we only need to work out the second.\n\nThe path $C_2$ is the line segment from $O$ to $B.$ It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{2}(t) & =\\mathbf{b}+t(\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{b}) \\\\  & =⟨0,0⟩+t(⟨1,1⟩-⟨0,0⟩) \\\\  & =⟨t,t⟩ \\\\  & =t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0 \\lt t \\lt 1.$ Along this segment, we have $x = t$ and $y = t.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}({\\mathbf{r}}_{2}(t)) & =(2x-3y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(2y-5x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =(2t-3t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(2t-5t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =-t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-3t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{2}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}({\\mathbf{r}}_{2}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}_{2}^{'}(t) & =(-t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-3t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot (\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =-t\\cdot 1+(-3t)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-4t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & =-4+{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\mathbf{F}({\\mathbf{r}}_{\\mathbf{2}}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}_{2}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4+{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-4t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4+[-2{t}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-4+[-2+0] \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186179525 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int\\limits_{t_a}^{t_b}  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(0,3)$ to $(2,3).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $\\mathbf{a}=3\\,\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=2\\,\\mathbf{i}+3\\,\\mathbf{j}.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =\\mathbf{a}+t\\hspace{0.167em}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\  & =3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+t[(2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})-3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}] \\\\  & =3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+t(2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}) \\\\  & =2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0 \\le t \\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=2t$ and $y=3$ along the segment.\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = e^{x+y},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & ={e}^{2t+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{e}^{x+y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{2t+3}\\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{2t+3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=2t+3,$ $\\textrm d u=2\\,\\textrm d t$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{2t+3}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{3}^{5}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\,{|}_{3}^{5} \\\\  & ={e}^{5}-{e}^{3} \\\\  & ={e}^{3}({e}^{2}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186179527 "First, notice that we can represent $C,$ which is traversed in clockwise, as the union of $3$ paths:\n\n$$\nC = \\{-C_1\\} \\cup \\{-C_2\\} \\cup C_3,\n$$\n\nwhere $-C_1$ and $-C_2$ are the same paths as $C_1$ and $C_2$ but traversed in the opposite direction.\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}+{\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186179529 "First, notice that a parametrization for the given path is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =x(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=2\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\\hspace{1em}t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)$  is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & ={x}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{y}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=-2\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{y\\,dx-x\\,dy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{y\\cdot {x}^{′}-x\\cdot {y}^{′}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)-(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}},dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{-4{sin}^{2}⁡t-4{cos}^{2}⁡t}{4({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{-4({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)}{4({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}\\,dt \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,dt \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186179530 "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y = x^2.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) &=x(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\\hspace{2em}t\\in [0,1]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=\\mathbf{i}+2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(x+y)\\,dx+(x-y)\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+(x-y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}((x+y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+(x-y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(t+{t}^{2})\\cdot 1+(t-{t}^{2})\\cdot 2t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}t+3{t}^{2}-2{t}^{3}\\,dt \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+{t}^{3}-\\frac{{t}^{4}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{2}+1-\\frac{1}{2}]-[0] \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186179571 "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x = x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y = t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx = x(t) = t^2.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle t^2 , \\: t \\right\\rangle, \\qquad 1 \\leq t \\leq 3\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1 \\leq t \\leq 3$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the endpoints on the path.\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(2t)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{x}{y},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =\\frac{{t}^{2}}{t} \\\\  & =t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{x}{y}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}t\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186179576 "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $\\nabla f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{2x-3y}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x-3y} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{2x-3y}) \\\\  & =-3{e}^{2x-3y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x-3y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-3{e}^{2x-3y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186179580 "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x = x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = x(t) = \\ln{t}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle \\ln{t}, \\: t \\right\\rangle, \\qquad 1 \\leq t \\leq 2\\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1 \\leq t \\leq 2\\sqrt{2}$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the endpoints on the path.\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{t}^{2}+1}{{t}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}{|t|} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y) = y^3e^{-x},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & ={t}^{3}{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}t} \\\\  & ={t}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{t} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{y}^{3}{e}^{-x}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{2\\sqrt{2}}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2\\sqrt{2}}{t}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitution $u = t^2 + 1,$ $\\textrm{d}u = 2t \\, \\textrm{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{2}^{9}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}\\,{|}_{2}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\sqrt{{9}^{3}}-\\sqrt{{2}^{3}}] \\\\  & =\\frac{27-2\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186179581 "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $\\nabla f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-2x \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =4y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =-2x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+4y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(2,1),$ $(-2,1),$ and $(1,-1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $\\nabla f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(2,1) & =-2\\cdot 2\\,i+4\\cdot 1\\,j=-4\\,i+4\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-2,1) & =-2\\cdot (-2)\\,i+4\\cdot 1\\,j=4\\,i+4\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,-1) & =-2\\cdot 1\\,i+4\\cdot (-1)\\,j=-2\\,i-4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we draw the vectors at their corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/b8/b88c62b154cecaace206248b62f72b8aa1f71662c0427adfbbabd5c2b4738a6b.png)"] [17592186179586 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  =  \\int\\limits_{t_a}^{t_b}  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(1,0)$ to $(1,1).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $\\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =\\mathbf{a}+t\\hspace{0.167em}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+t[(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j})-\\mathbf{i}] \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0 \\le t \\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=1$ and $y=t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =0\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = x+\\sin{y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =1+\\operatorname{sin}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+\\operatorname{sin}t)\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+\\operatorname{sin}t)\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186179587 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}s  =  \\int\\limits_{t_a}^{t_b}  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(0,1,-3)$ to $(-1,2,-2).$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $\\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{j}-3\\mathbf{k}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=-\\,\\mathbf{i}+2\\,\\mathbf{j}-2\\,\\mathbf{k}.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =\\mathbf{a}+t\\hspace{0.167em}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{j}-3\\mathbf{k})+t[(-\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{j}-3\\mathbf{k})] \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{j}-3\\mathbf{k})+t(-\\mathbf{i}+\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(t+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(t-3)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0 \\le t \\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=-t, y=t+1,$ and $z=t-3$ along the segment.\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+1)\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-3)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y,z) = x^2+y-z$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =(-t)^{2}+(t+1)-(t-3) \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+4)\\cdot \\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+4)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186179590 "The position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $\\mathbf{a} = 0\\,\\mathbf{i} + 0\\,\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{b} = 1\\,\\mathbf{i} + 1\\,\\mathbf{j}.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =\\mathbf{a}+t\\hspace{0.167em}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\  & =t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0 \\le t \\le 1.$ So, we have that $x(t) = t$ and $y(t) = t$ along the segment.\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}y\\,dx+xy\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+xy\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+xy\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\cdot t\\cdot 1+t\\cdot t\\cdot 1\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{3}+{t}^{2}\\,dt \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{4}}{4}+\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186179600 "Given a piecewise-smooth path $C$ and a vector field \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = P(x,y) \\: \\mathbf{i} + Q(x,y) \\: \\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\ndefined on $C,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} = \\int\\limits_C P(x,y) \\: \\text{d}x + Q(x,y) \\: \\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $P = 3x+2$ and $Q = -y^3.$ Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{C}(3x+2)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x-{y}^{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186179958 "Use $y=t$. Then $x=-t^2/16$, with $-8\\le t\\le4$, and\n\n$$\nds=\\sqrt{\\left(-\\frac t8\\right)^2+1}\\,dt=\\frac{\\sqrt{t^2+64}}8\\,dt.\n$$\n\nAlso $32xy=32(-t^2/16)t=-2t^3$. Hence\n\n$$\n\\int_C32xy\\,ds=-\\frac14\\int_{-8}^{4}t^3\\sqrt{t^2+64}\\,dt.\n$$\n\nThe missing expression is $t^3\\sqrt{t^2+64}$."] [17592186179963 "Notice that the given curve can be expressed in the form $x=x(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=8x\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=x(t)=\\frac{{t}^{2}}{8}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨\\frac{{t}^{2}}{8},\\hspace{0.222em}t⟩,\\hspace{2em}0\\le t\\le 3\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $0\\le t\\le 3$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{8}\\right)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{t}{4}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{t}{4}\\right)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{t}^{2}}{16}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=\\frac{24x}{y}$, then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =\\frac{24x}{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{24\\cdot \\left(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{8}\\right)}{t} \\\\  & =3t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{24x}{y}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}3t\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{3}t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve this using the substitution $u={t}^{2}+16,$ $\\text{d}u=2t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{3}t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{3}{8}{\\int }_{16}^{25}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{16}^{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}({25}^{3/2}-{16}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(125-64) \\\\  & =\\frac{61}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186179964 "Differentiate the scalar function with respect to each coordinate:\n\n$$\nf_x=2x-y,\\qquad f_y=-x+6y-4.\n$$\n\nThus its gradient vector field is\n\n$$\n\\nabla f=(2x-y)i+(6y-x-4)j.\n$$"] [17592186179965 "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z-2y)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z-2y) \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z-2y)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2x}{z-2y} \\\\ {f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z-2y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{z-2y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y,z)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}(z-2y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{2x}{z-2y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{x}{z-2y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186179966 "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-y) \\\\  & =2x \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-y) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =2x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-2,1),$ $(0,2),$ and $(2,-1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-2,1) & =2\\cdot (-2)\\,i-j=-4\\,i-j \\\\ ∇f(0,2) & =2\\cdot (0)\\,i-j=-\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,-1) & =2\\cdot 2\\,i-j=4\\,i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/dc/dc36c0bfd1e089dc4e5017b4d8eb3fc342aff35ca6b8ce1943a209915592d83c.png)"] [17592186179969 "With $y=t$, the curve is $r(t)=\\langle2t^3,t\\rangle$, $0\\le t\\le2$. Thus\n\n$$\nds=\\sqrt{(6t^2)^2+1}\\,dt=\\sqrt{36t^4+1}\\,dt,\n\\qquad xy^2=2t^5.\n$$\n\nConsequently\n\n$$\n\\int_Cxy^2\\,ds=2\\int_0^2t^5\\sqrt{36t^4+1}\\,dt.\n$$"] [17592186179972 "Choose the smooth parametrization $x=t$, $y=t^2$, $0\\le t\\le\\sqrt2$. Then $ds=\\sqrt{1+4t^2}\\,dt$ and $4xy=4t^3$, so\n\n$$\n\\int_C4xy\\,ds=\\int_0^{\\sqrt2}4t^3\\sqrt{1+4t^2}\\,dt.\n$$\n\nLet $u=1+4t^2$. The bounds become $1$ and $9$, and $4t^3dt=(u-1)du/8$. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C4xy\\,ds&=\\frac18\\int_1^9(u-1)u^{1/2}\\,du\\\\\n&=\\frac18\\left[\\frac25u^{5/2}-\\frac23u^{3/2}\\right]_1^9\n=\\frac{149}{15}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186179973 "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}-2{y}^{2}}{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}-2{y}^{2}}{4}\\right) \\\\  & =-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-2,2),$ $(2,-3),$ and $(2,1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-2,2) & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot (-2)^{2}\\,i-2\\,j=3\\,i-2\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,-3) & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot {2}^{2}\\,i-(-3)\\,j=3\\,i+3\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,1) & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot {2}^{2}\\,i-\\,j=3\\,i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/26/26a5c0123d93231a800456e9f06f6ba7450861838c87d4d1b3ae77dab4cec6cf.png)"] [17592186179981 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(0,2)$ to $(3,1).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $\\mathbf{a}=2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =\\mathbf{a}+t\\hspace{0.167em}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\  & =2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+t[(3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j})-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}] \\\\  & =2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+t(3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =3t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(2-t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=3t$ and $y=2-t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =(3t)^{2}-(2-t) \\\\  & =9{t}^{2}+t-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}+t-2)\\cdot \\sqrt{10}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}+t-2)\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186179982 "Parametrize the segment by\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle1+t,3t,1-t\\rangle,\\qquad0\\le t\\le1.\n$$\n\nThe speed is $|r'(t)|=\\sqrt{1+9+1}=\\sqrt{11}$, and $f(r(t))=2+3t$. Thus\n\n$$\n\\int_C f\\,ds=\\sqrt{11}\\int_0^1(2+3t)\\,dt.\n$$"] [17592186179986 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(1,3)$ to $(0,3).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $\\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{i}+3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =\\mathbf{a}+t\\hspace{0.167em}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}+3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})+t[3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-(\\mathbf{i}+3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})] \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}+3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})-t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i} \\\\  & =(1-t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=1-t$ and $y=3$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =\\operatorname{ln}((1-t)+3) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}(4-t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}(4-t)\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}(4-t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186180008 "Since $C$ is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =x(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\\hspace{2em}t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=-\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{y}^{2}\\,dx-{x}^{2}\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{y}^{2}\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t-{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}({y}^{2}\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}-{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}-{sin}^{3}⁡t-{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t+{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186180009 "The position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $\\mathbf{a}=-\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =\\mathbf{a}+t\\hspace{0.167em}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}+t(2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-(-\\mathbf{i})) \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}+t(3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =(3t-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+4t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x(t)=3t-1$ and $y(t)=4t$ along the segment.\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}y\\,dx-x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t-x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4t(3)-(3t-1)(4)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4\\,dt \\\\  & =4t\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =4(1-0) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186180017 "Along the segment from $A$ to $B$, both the field arrows and the direction of traversal point right. Their dot product is positive, so\n\n$$\n\\int_C F\\cdot dr>0.\n$$\n\nReversing the path changes the sign of a vector line integral:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-C}F\\cdot dr=-\\int_C F\\cdot dr<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore statement I only is true."] [17592186180018 "Given a piecewise-smooth path $C$ and a vector field \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=P(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{i}+Q(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{j}+R(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{k}\n$$\n\ndefined on $C,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{C}P(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x+Q(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y+R(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{C}{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x-{x}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y+5\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186180019 "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $\\mathbf{F}$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion  of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is false, while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ is true. Since the force $\\mathbf{F}$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion. \n\n![](images/20/20121d30445c9e756f1c2c024713bc6cd6f0464cfcfc24df68bc30c766c4988e.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ is true. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $\\mathbf{F}$ is similar to the previous case.\n\n![](images/0f/0ff98536c275458d68deabda91f56bf63e7c8f59f5ea79c68292332d8cd58d4f.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0;\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ only."] [17592186180020 "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $\\mathbf{F}$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is false while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$ is true. It's clear from the picture below that $\\mathbf{F}$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/4c/4c11b38f9e598e0cd8fc50715d2091e5bea48d188440ae8a99c84d66a6acb6b9.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is true. It's clear from the picture below that $\\mathbf{F}$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/34/346007ae582fccdf4519a3d1cd50a467e19d5345238343a1311fd3348bbeb138.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0;\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ only."] [17592186180024 "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $\\mathbf{F}$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is false, while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$ is true. It's clear from the picture below that $\\mathbf{F}$ works against the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/d3/d363cf00c4901fa0c29bb9521cdd2ea3c12c377dd8e38306df0d5a2837915a36.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is true. It's clear from the picture below that if we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then $\\mathbf{F}$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/c5/c5799e631e7eb45ec5a9711ef1569d51b376d8c764a3226caa2676cdb968a6f5.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0;\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ only."] [17592186180032 "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $\\mathbf{F}$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/a7/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](images/3a/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement “The flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is positive” is false, while statement “The flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is zero” is true. From the diagram below we see that $\\mathbf{F}$ acts perpendicular to $\\mathbf{n}$ at every point on $C.$ Therefore, there is no net flow of $\\mathbf{F}$ through $C.$\n\n![](images/d7/d7a469a331a3cda238fcefb9e7163743483975c4ecc7b962d6d5f8d79736693c.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement “The flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $-C$ is negative” is false. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](images/29/29f144d6f3ce8233a034025fe933bc57dbea5476c50a259853a98bd2a2b3a47b.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $-C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is zero” only."] [17592186180034 "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\n![](images/38/386f010932b5846e7f47a605a94a5b5d580bd35dbe37512b1583d15d29f3dc70.png)\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}({x}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot (x\\mathbf{i}+y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}{x}^{3}-{y}^{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is ${x}^{3}-{y}^{3}.$"] [17592186180035 "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nIn our case, we need to find an expression for the flux across only the part ${C}_{1}$ of the closed curve $C.$ Since this side is perpendicular to the $x$-axis and we require an outward-pointing unit normal vector, we must have $\\mathbf{n}=\\mathbf{i}.$\n\n![](images/c4/c4c4f2f3d5b6188ac5a6ec41bb96fc0c7132cc39308cebfa16ee759eabac2c75.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}({x}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot \\mathbf{i}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}{x}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is ${x}^{2}.$"] [17592186180036 "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{4}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${\\mathbf{n}}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/3b/3bd953b7fb02b90a55a29bea6f58ff9bd8bab77d62b1df1c547ca899656d37a3.png)\n\nNow, since the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is parallel to ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{3},$ there is zero net flow through these two sides. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nSo, our expression for the flux reduces to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{4}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nNow, since the vector field is uniform, the net outflow through ${C}_{2}$ will cancel with the net inflow through ${C}_{4}.$ That is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s=-{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{4}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s\\hspace{1em}⟹\\hspace{1em}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{4}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s=0.\n$$"] [17592186180083 "For the stream-function convention $F=\\psi_y i-\\psi_x j$, differentiate\n\n$$\n\\psi_y=2x\\cos y-e^y,\\qquad\\psi_x=2\\sin y.\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=(2x\\cos y-e^y)i-2\\sin y\\,j.\n$$\n\nIts divergence is $2\\cos y-2\\cos y=0$, as expected for a stream function."] [17592186180084 "A vector field $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each vector field.\n\n- Vector field $F(x,y)=y\\,i-x\\,j$ has a stream function. Indeed, the partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=0,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0+0=0\n$$\n\n- Vector field $F(x,y)=xy\\,i+{y}^{2}\\,j$ doesn't have a stream function. The partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=y+2y\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Vector field $F(x,y)=2x\\,i-y\\,j$ doesn't have a stream function. The partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=2+(-1)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $F(x,y)=y\\,i-x\\,j$ only."] [17592186180091 "The streamlines of a vector field are the level curves of its stream function and are tangential to the vector field at every point. \n\nIf $\\mathbf{F}$ represents the velocity field of a fluid flow, then they show the path traced out by a massless particle as it moves with the flow.\n. \n\nA vector field $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)={x}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-2xy\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D={\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ where $P(x,y)={x}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=-2xy.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\mathbf{F}$ has a stream function, since\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=2x+(-2x)=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we find a stream function. A function $ψ(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ if \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y},\\,Q=-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y}={x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}=2xy.\n$$\n\nIntegrating these equations, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)={x}^{2}y+f(x),\\,ψ(x,y)={x}^{2}y+g(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are arbitrary functions. Finally, setting $f=g=0,$ we have\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)={x}^{2}y.\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the level curve of the stream function $ψ(x,y)={x}^{2}y$ that passes through $P.$\nFirst, we find the value of the stream function at $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ψ(-1,-1) & =(-1)^{2}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the streamline that passes through $P(-1,-1)$ is the $-1$-level curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ψ(x,y) & =-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}y & =-1 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the streamline that passes through $P(-1,-1)$ is $y=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.$\nThe vector field and the streamline through the point $P$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/b8/b803554d852d985c8feca61804a9e52423391fc4624a01c4199d0744fbf2e28d.png)"] [17592186180173 "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}.\n$$\n\nWe're given the value of the first integral, so we only need to work out the second.\n\nThe path ${C}_{2}$ is the line segment from $A$ to $B.$ It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{2}(t) & =\\mathbf{a}+t(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\  & =⟨2,0⟩+t(⟨1,1⟩-⟨2,0⟩) \\\\  & =⟨2-t,t⟩ \\\\  & =(2-t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0<t<1.$ Along this segment, we have $x=2-t$ and $y=t.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}({\\mathbf{r}}_{2}(t)) & =(x+y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(x-y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =(2-t+t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(2-t-t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(2-2t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{2}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}({\\mathbf{r}}_{2}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}_{2}^{'}(t) & =(2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(2-2t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot (-\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =-2+(2-2t) \\\\  & =-2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & =2+{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\mathbf{F}({\\mathbf{r}}_{\\mathbf{2}}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}_{2}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2+{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-2t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2-{t}^{2}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2-(1-0) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186180175 "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}.\n$$\n\nWe're given the value of the second integral, so we only need to work out the first.\n\n${C}_{1}$ is the section of parabola $y={x}^{2}$ from $O$ to $A.$ It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{1}(t) &=t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0<t<2.$ Along this curve, we have $x=t$ and $y={t}^{2}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}({\\mathbf{r}}_{1}(t)) & =x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{1}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}({\\mathbf{r}}_{1}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}_{1}^{'}(t) & =(t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot (\\mathbf{i}+2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =t+2{t}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\mathbf{F}({\\mathbf{r}}_{\\mathbf{1}}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}_{1}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t-2 \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(t+2{t}^{3})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t-2 \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{t}^{4}}{2}]}_{0}^{2}-2 \\\\  & =[2+8]-2 \\\\  & =10-2 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186180177 "Parametrize the segment by $x=1+t$, $y=2-t$, $0\\le t\\le1$. Its speed is $\\sqrt2$. Hence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C2xy\\,ds\n&=2\\sqrt2\\int_0^1(1+t)(2-t)\\,dt\\\\\n&=2\\sqrt2\\left[2t+\\frac{t^2}2-\\frac{t^3}3\\right]_0^1\n=\\frac{13\\sqrt2}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186180278 "Use $r(t)=\\langle1+t,2t,1+2t\\rangle$, $0\\le t\\le1$. Its speed is $3$, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi y}4\\right)\\,ds\n&=3\\int_0^1\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}2\\right)\\,dt\\\\\n&=-\\frac6\\pi\\left[\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}2\\right)\\right]_0^1\n=\\frac6\\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186180279 "First, notice that we can represent $C,$ which is traversed in counterclockwise, as the union of $3$ paths:\n\n$$\nC={C}_{1}\\cup \\{-{C}_{2}\\}\\cup {C}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $-{C}_{2}$ is the same path as ${C}_{2}$ but traversed in the opposite direction.\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}+{\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186180280 "First, notice that we can represent $C,$ which is traversed clockwise, as the union of $3$ paths:\n\n$$\nC=\\{-{C}_{1}\\}\\cup {C}_{2}\\cup \\{-{C}_{3}\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $-{C}_{1}$ and $-{C}_{3}$ are the same paths as ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{3}$ but traversed in the opposite direction.\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}+{\\int }_{-{C}_{3}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186180281 "The segment lies on $y=0$. Parametrize it by $x=1-2t$, $y=0$, $0\\le t\\le1$, giving $ds=2\\,dt$. Then\n\n$$\n\\int_C(2x-3y)\\,ds=\\int_0^1(2-4t)\\,2\\,dt\n=\\left[4t-4t^2\\right]_0^1=0.\n$$\n\nThe positive and negative contributions cancel. The arc-length factor is positive even though $x$ decreases."] [17592186180284 "The position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $\\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =\\mathbf{a}+t\\hspace{0.167em}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{j}+t(3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{j}+t(3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =3t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(1+2t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x(t)=3t$ and $y(t)=1+2t$ along the segment.\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}\\,dx-y\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t-y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3t)^{2}\\cdot 3-(1+2t)\\cdot 2\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}27{t}^{2}-2-4t\\,dt \\\\  & ={[9{t}^{3}-2t-2{t}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[9(1)^{3}-2(1)-2(1)^{2}]-[0] \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186180286 "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y=(x+1)^{2}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) &=x(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(t+1)^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\\hspace{2em}t\\in [0,1]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=\\mathbf{i}+2(t+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{dx+dy}{y} & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{y}(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{y}(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1+2(t+1)}{(t+1)^{2}}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{(t+1)^{2}}+\\frac{2}{t+1}\\,dt \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{t+1}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[-\\frac{1}{1+1}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+1)]-[-\\frac{1}{0+1}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0+1)] \\\\  & =[-\\frac{1}{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2]-[-1+0] \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186180287 "Since $C$ is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =x(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\\hspace{2em}t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=-\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{y\\,dy-x\\,dx}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{y\\cdot {y}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t-x\\cdot {x}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{y\\cdot {y}^{′}-x\\cdot {x}^{′}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}{{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}[-1-1] \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186198016 "Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}: {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u \\\\ v\\end{array} \\right]} \n\\longrightarrow \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x ( u , v ) \\\\ y ( u , v )\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(x(u,v), y(u,v)) \\:  \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right|  \\ \\mathrm d u \\mathrm d v\n$$\n\nwhere $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y) =  (2x+y)y^2\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=2x+y,\n\\qquad\nv=y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}^{-1}:{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x \\\\ y\\end{array} \\right]} \\to {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u ( x , y ) \\\\ v ( x , y )\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $\\Delta,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}(\\frac{3}{2},0) & =(2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}+0,0) \\\\  & =(3,0) \\\\ {\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}(\\frac{5}{2},0) & =(2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}+0,0) \\\\  & =(5,0) \\\\ {\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}(1,3) & =(2\\cdot 1+3,3) \\\\  & =(5,3) \\\\ {\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}(0,3) & =(2\\cdot 0+3,3) \\\\  & =(3,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 3 \\leq u \\leq 5, \\quad 0 \\leq v \\leq 3 \\big\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/c2/c296b1ee0451686608ec4b06c29d114ff1918233886d8471d3b2c118851aa25e.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T.$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} \\right)^{-1} =\\dfrac12\n$$\n\nNote that $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\neq 0$ everywhere inside $\\Delta.$ In other words, $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral.\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(2x+y){y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}u{v}^{2}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}u{v}^{2}\\,|\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{3}^{5}u{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}v{\\int }_{3}^{5}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{3}{v}^{3}]}_{0}^{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{3}^{5}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 9\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186198020 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v) \\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S\n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\cdot (\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v) \\,\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}({v}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2u,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}({v}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}2v,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2u & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2v & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 2v & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2v\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}4uv⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =⟨x+y,\\hspace{0.222em}x-y,\\hspace{0.222em}xyz⟩{|}_{x={u}^{2}\\,y={v}^{2}\\,z=1} \\\\  & =⟨{u}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{u}^{2}-{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{u}^{2}{v}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and $\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨{u}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{u}^{2}-{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{u}^{2}{v}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}4uv⟩ \\\\  & =({u}^{2}+{v}^{2})\\cdot 0+({u}^{2}-{v}^{2})\\cdot 0+{u}^{2}{v}^{2}\\cdot 4uv \\\\  & =4{u}^{3}{v}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}4{u}^{3}{v}^{3}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186198022 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v) \\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S\n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\cdot (\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v) \\,\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(1+2u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(1+2u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix},-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,\\hspace{0.222em}3,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & ={⟨z,\\hspace{0.222em}x,\\hspace{0.222em}y⟩|}_{x=1+2u+v\\,y=u+v\\,z=u-v} \\\\  & =⟨u-v,\\hspace{0.222em}1+2u+v,\\hspace{0.222em}u+v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and $\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨u-v,\\hspace{0.222em}1+2u+v,\\hspace{0.222em}u+v⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,\\hspace{0.222em}3,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩ \\\\  & =(u-v)\\cdot (-2)+(1+2u+v)\\cdot 3+(u+v)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-2u+2v+3+6u+3v+u+v \\\\  & =5u+6v+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨z,\\hspace{0.222em}x,\\hspace{0.222em}y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(5u+6v+3)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(5u+6v+3)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}v\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[5uv+3{v}^{2}+3v]_{v=0}^{v=1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(5u+6)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={[\\frac{5}{2}{u}^{2}+6u]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =10+12 \\\\  & =22.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186198579 "Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{(x+y)^{2}}{2x+y}\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=2x+y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}(0,1) & =(0+1,2(0)+1) \\\\  & =(1,1) \\\\ {\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}(1,0) & =(1+0,2(1)+0) \\\\  & =(1,2) \\\\ {\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}(-1,3) & =(-1+3,2(-1)+3) \\\\  & =(2,1) \\\\ {\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}(0,2) & =(0+2,2(0)+2) \\\\  & =(2,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(u,v)\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}1\\le u\\le 2,\\hspace{1em}1\\le v\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/3a/3a831bbc56c0ce5e749fcf45bd65825f297d7732158b8f9e371f2178e61b92c0.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=-1\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $\\mathbf{T}$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{(x+y)^{2}}{2x+y}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{{u}^{2}}{v}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{{u}^{2}}{v}\\,|-1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{{u}^{2}}{v}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{{u}^{2}}{v}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & {[\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3}]}_{1}^{2}\\cdot {[\\operatorname{ln}⁡v]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186198585 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice we are told that the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}=⟨8uv,\\hspace{0.222em}4uv,\\hspace{0.222em}4uv⟩.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =⟨2x+y,\\hspace{0.222em}2z,\\hspace{0.222em}y+z⟩{|}_{x=-{u}^{2}\\,y=2{u}^{2}-{v}^{2}\\,z={v}^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨2(-{u}^{2})+(2{u}^{2}-{v}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}2({v}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}(2{u}^{2}-{v}^{2})+{v}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}2{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}2{u}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨-{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}2{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}2{u}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨8uv,\\hspace{0.222em}4uv,\\hspace{0.222em}4uv⟩ \\\\  & =(-{v}^{2})\\cdot 8uv+2{v}^{2}\\cdot 4uv+2{u}^{2}\\cdot 4uv \\\\  & =-8u{v}^{3}+8u{v}^{3}+8{u}^{3}v \\\\  & =8{u}^{3}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}8{u}^{3}v}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]}
